    readback of the native map or observed work counts for every GPU ray. Root chord, occupied length,
    disjoint intervals, uniform-64 support hits, count/spacing and projection footprint are separate.
    Day footprint is 48.027x19.442 world units with maximum selected occupied length 124.66;
    grazing is 48.027x.751 with maximum occupied 4631.58 versus root chord 10371.91. The latter's
    uniform-64 spacing can exceed 162 world units, versus 50-world-unit native columns. Layered and
    thin selected chords reach 10000, with a 300-unit gap in the layered field. Segment storage cannot
    recover matter absent from sampled density; exact support discovery has its own measurable cost.
  Allocation -> production policy 1 estimates horizontal column visits conservatively from the
    root-clipped chord: 1+ceil(abs(light.x)*length/50)+ceil(abs(light.z)*length/50). Estimates above 16
    retain the original bounded uniform generator without scanning columns. Eligible shorter rays
    measure native occupied arc length, then sample min(cap,max(1,ceil(occupiedLength/50))) midpoints
    on that arc. The cap remains 64 per complete texel ray, not per column. Each sample's weight is
    divided across its represented native intervals and depth bins; gaps contribute no weight.
    The estimate selects uniform fallback in 23/25 sampled grazing centers and all sampled layered/
    thin centers. Selected day/night centers need at most three density evaluations, sparse four.
    These sampled counts are derived diagnostics; GPU work bounds are checked independently.
  Support and units -> exact-zero cache-local slab clipping covers tiny nonzero horizontal and
    vertical crossings without altering primary/reference helpers. Each native-support pass has a
    defensive 401-column limit (802 queries per center ray before density-neighbor queries). Failed
    walks publish unavailable records and select bounded marching. Zero procedural samples cannot
    certify native support empty; metadata retains the geometric chord. World-length weights use
    the existing metres conversion. Source selection, publication/barriers/lifetimes, noise anchoring,
    weather, user controls, primary/secondary schedules, OIT and temporal policy remain unchanged.
  Footprint decision -> initial/ contains separate pure-support-center and two-area-ray trials.
    The area variant uses opposite quarter-texel points, independent clipping/support and equal
    density weights, splitting the same 64-evaluation cap into at most 32 per ray. It averages density
    integrals, not transmissions, and loses axial resolution; two points are not exact area filtering.
